issues.problematic_code_snippet:
// Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out
await Task.Yield();

var state = await AuthenticationProvider.GetAuthenticationStateAsync();

private async Task ProcessLogOut(string returnUrl)
{
    if (!ValidateSignOutRequestState())
    {
        Log.LogoutOperationInitiatedExternally(Logger);
        Navigation.NavigateTo(ApplicationPaths.LogOutFailedPath, AuthenticationNavigationOptions with { HistoryEntryState = "The logout was not initiated from within the page." });
        return;
    }

public void Redirect()
{
    if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null)
    {
        _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
    }
    else
    {
        _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl!);
    }

findings.body:
`ProcessLogOut` used to run the legacy session-storage check only when `HistoryEntryState` was null. It now calls `ValidateSignOutRequestState()` unconditionally. That helper returns `GetCachedNavigationState()?.Interaction == InteractionType.SignOut`, which is false whenever the history state is null. So a logout with no history state always goes to `LogOutFailedPath`.

The PR description says the change is "only fail validation when state exists but is invalid." The code does the opposite. Nothing in the diff covers the null-state branch: the one test for this path was deleted, and no new test replaces it.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="279-292"}

::callout{type="warning"}
If the intent is "legacy sign-out without history state is no longer supported," say so in the description and add a test that asserts the failure redirect. If the intent is the description's version, the guard needs to be restored for the null case.

::file{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/test/RemoteAuthenticatorCoreTests.cs"}

The old condition was `InteractionOptions != null && InteractiveRequestUrl != null`. That check also narrowed the nullable `InteractiveRequestUrl` for the compiler. The new condition checks only `InteractionOptions`, and the else branch dereferences `InteractiveRequestUrl!`.

There are two effects:
- `NavigateToLogin(string loginPath, ...)` takes a non-nullable parameter, and `InteractiveRequestUrl` is `string?`. This is likely to produce CS8604 under the repo's warnings-as-errors policy, which would break the build.
- `AccessTokenResult`'s 4-argument constructor is public and accepts a null URL with non-null options. The old code handled that case by falling back to `RedirectUrl`. The new code throws `ArgumentNullException` from `NavigateToLogin` or `NavigateTo`.

The built-in `RequestAccessToken` always sets both fields together, so the framework's own paths do not hit this. Custom providers that construct `AccessTokenResult` directly can.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/Services/AccessTokenNotAvailableException.cs" lines="38-46"}

::suggestion{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/Services/AccessTokenNotAvailableException.cs" lines="40-45"}
if (_tokenResult.InteractionOptions != null && _tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl != null)
{
    _navigation.NavigateToLogin(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl, _tokenResult.InteractionOptions);
}
else if (_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl != null)
{
    _navigation.NavigateTo(_tokenResult.InteractiveRequestUrl);
}

The PR adds `await Task.Yield();` in `ProcessLogOut` with the comment "Ensure we're authenticated before proceeding with sign-out." The next line already checks `IsAuthenticated`, and `Task.Yield()` does not check authentication. It only defers the continuation.

A yield inside a Blazor dispatcher can change ordering in ways that are hard to reason about. It looks like a workaround for a race or a test timing issue, but the PR doesn't say so, and nothing in the diff shows what it fixes. If it is needed, the comment should explain why. If it isn't, it should be removed.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.Authentication/src/RemoteAuthenticatorViewCore.cs" lines="288-293"}

`InternalCalls.InvokeJS` is deleted. Its own comment says it "only exists for backwards compatibility" and that its namespace, type and method name "must match the corresponding entries in driver.c in the Mono distribution." The PR removes it without addressing that. Nothing in the repo calls it (I grepped for `InvokeJS<`), so the managed side is safe. The open question is whether the WebAssembly runtime still looks this symbol up by name, for example when a newer managed assembly runs on an older runtime pack.

::diff{file="src/Components/WebAssembly/WebAssembly.JSInterop/src/InternalCalls.cs" lines="8-11"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The obsolete policy covers public API, and this is internal. Still, the comment makes a runtime-compatibility claim that the PR does not address. Please get sign-off from the WebAssembly runtime owners.
